  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Bram van Leeuwen was a Dutch entrepreneur who built a group spanning correspondence education, publishing and mail-order retail. Raised in poverty in Rotterdam, he founded the Nederlands Talen Instituut and Lekturama, then sold his businesses to Vendex. He later supported charitable work for older people and children in his home city.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 91,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Company history, foundation record and independent business histories converge."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S2",
      "S3",
      "S4",
      "S5"
    ],
    "word_count": 51,
    "html": "<p>Bram van Leeuwen was a Dutch entrepreneur who built a group spanning correspondence education, publishing and mail-order retail. Raised in poverty in Rotterdam, he founded the Nederlands Talen Instituut and Lekturama, then sold his businesses to Vendex. He later supported charitable work for older people and children in his home city.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "When his Nederlands Talen Instituut opened its Rotterdam headquarters in 1967, Bram van Leeuwen used the occasion to extend language learning beyond paying students. On behalf of the Nederlands Onderwijsfonds, he offered English courses with sound recordings for institutions and libraries serving blind people in the Netherlands and Belgium.\n\nRotterdam was also the focus of his grantmaking foundation. He established the Van Leeuwen Van Lignac Stichting in 1995 to support physical and mental wellbeing in Rotterdam. Its priorities included isolated older residents and young children in care homes or hospitals. Part of his estate passed to the foundation after his death, providing resources for its grantmaking.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 92,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Contemporaneous opening report and primary foundation purpose establish providers, beneficiaries and funding mechanism."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S2",
      "S4"
    ],
    "word_count": 106,
    "html": "<p>When his Nederlands Talen Instituut opened its Rotterdam headquarters in 1967, Bram van Leeuwen used the occasion to extend language learning beyond paying students. On behalf of the Nederlands Onderwijsfonds, he offered English courses with sound recordings for institutions and libraries serving blind people in the Netherlands and Belgium.</p>\r\n<p>Rotterdam was also the focus of his grantmaking foundation. He established the Van Leeuwen Van Lignac Stichting in 1995 to support physical and mental wellbeing in Rotterdam. Its priorities included isolated older residents and young children in care homes or hospitals. Part of his estate passed to the foundation after his death, providing resources for its grantmaking.</p>\r\n"
  },
